\section{Setting up the diagnostic quiz, literately}
\label{app:quiz}

This appendix is a literate program \autocite{LiterateProgramming}: the
prose and the code are one and the same source, woven into the text you
are reading and tangled into the files the computer runs.
It implements the quizzes of \cref{prior-knowledge,end-measure}: it sets
them up in the learning platform, and it prepares their results for the
analysis.
Four files are tangled from this section: [[quiz-background.json]],
[[quiz-knowledge-start.json]] and [[quiz-knowledge-end.json]], the
descriptions of the quizzes that the [[canvaslms]] tool pushes to
Canvas, and [[analyze_quiz.py]], which fetches the results from Canvas,
summarises them, and prepares the open-text answers for coding.

\subsection{The three quizzes for Canvas}

The learning platform of the course is Canvas, and the [[canvaslms]]
command-line tool creates the quizzes from their JSON descriptions:
\begin{minted}{bash}
canvaslms quizzes create -c prgi26 -f quiz-background.json
canvaslms quizzes create -c prgi26 -f quiz-knowledge-start.json
canvaslms quizzes create -c prgi26 -f quiz-knowledge-end.json
\end{minted}
There are three quizzes.
The background quiz and the start-of-course knowledge quiz together
form the diagnostic of \cref{prior-knowledge}; the end-of-course
knowledge quiz repeats the knowledge questions to measure the gain
(\cref{end-measure}).
The knowledge test is separate from the background quiz because the two
differ in kind: the knowledge questions have right and wrong answers,
so that quiz can be graded---and the students may well want to know how
they did---whereas the background items have no right answers and
nothing to grade.
The knowledge items are defined once and appear in both knowledge
quizzes.

<<[[quiz-background.json]]>>=
{
  "quiz_type": "new",
  "settings": {
    "title": "Diagnostic quiz: your programming background",
    "instructions": "<p>This quiz maps your programming background so that we can adapt the course. It is not graded and there are no wrong answers. Please answer on your own, without help from others or from tools.</p>",
    "points_possible": 0,
    <<common quiz settings>>
    <<ungraded result view>>
  }
  },
  "items": [
    <<background items>>
  ]
}
@

<<[[quiz-knowledge-start.json]]>>=
{
  "quiz_type": "new",
  "settings": {
    "title": "Concept quiz: what you know already",
    "instructions": "<p>This quiz checks which programming concepts you already know, so that we can adapt the course. You will see your score afterwards, but not the correct answers&mdash;the course will teach you those. The score does not affect your grade. Please answer on your own, without help from others or from tools.</p>",
    "points_possible": 7,
    <<common quiz settings>>
    <<graded result view>>
  }
  },
  "items": [
    <<knowledge-part items>>
  ]
}
@

The end-of-course knowledge quiz opens with a question of its own:
whether the student prepared for the quiz.
We ask because preparation would confound the knowledge-gain measure of
\cref{end-measure}---a student who reviews the material the night
before measures preparation, not what the course left behind.

<<[[quiz-knowledge-end.json]]>>=
{
  "quiz_type": "new",
  "settings": {
    "title": "Concept quiz: what you know now",
    "instructions": "<p>This quiz repeats the concept questions from the start-of-course quiz, so that we can see what you have learned during the course. You will see your score afterwards, but not the correct answers. The score does not affect your grade. Please answer on your own, without preparation, help or tools.</p>",
    "points_possible": 7,
    <<common quiz settings>>
    <<graded result view>>
  }
  },
  "items": [
    <<end-quiz preparation item>>,
    <<knowledge-part items>>
  ]
}
@

All three quizzes are diagnostics: there is no time limit, a single
attempt suffices, and answers are never shuffled (order is meaningful
in several items).

<<common quiz settings>>=
"time_limit": null,
"due_at": null,
"unlock_at": null,
"lock_at": null,
"quiz_settings": {
  "shuffle_answers": false,
  "shuffle_questions": false,
  "has_time_limit": false,
  "one_at_a_time_type": "none",
  "allow_backtracking": true,
  "multiple_attempts_enabled": false,
  "result_view_restricted": true,
  "display_items": true,
  "display_item_response": true,
@

The quizzes differ in what the result view reveals.
The background quiz has nothing to grade, so it shows no points and no
correctness.
The knowledge quizzes show the points awarded---how well the student
did---but neither per-item correctness nor the correct answers: the
same questions must measure the gain at the end of the course
(\cref{end-measure}), so they have to stay fresh.

<<ungraded result view>>=
  "display_item_response_correctness": false,
  "display_item_correct_answer": false,
  "display_points_awarded": false,
  "display_points_possible": false
@

<<graded result view>>=
  "display_item_response_correctness": false,
  "display_item_correct_answer": false,
  "display_points_awarded": true,
  "display_points_possible": true
